## Solution

Consider the integral

$$I=\int_{1}^{2}\int_{1}^{2}\int_{1}^{2}\int_{1}^{2}\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}-x_{3}-x_{4}}{x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}}\,dx_{1}\,dx_{2}\,dx_{3}\,dx_{4}.$$

**Symmetry argument.** The domain of integration is the hypercube $[1,2]^{4}$, which is invariant under the involution

$$\sigma:(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4})\longmapsto(x_{3},x_{4},x_{1},x_{2}).$$

This map simply swaps the pair $(x_{1},x_{2})$ with the pair $(x_{3},x_{4})$. It is a measure-preserving bijection of $[1,2]^{4}$ onto itself (a permutation of coordinates, hence unit Jacobian).

Under $\sigma$:
- The **denominator** is unchanged:
$$x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}\;\longmapsto\;x_{3}+x_{4}+x_{1}+x_{2}=x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}+x_{4}.$$
- The **numerator** changes sign:
$$x_{1}+x_{2}-x_{3}-x_{4}\;\longmapsto\;x_{3}+x_{4}-x_{1}-x_{2}=-(x_{1}+x_{2}-x_{3}-x_{4}).$$

Therefore the integrand $f(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4})$ satisfies

$$f\circ\sigma=-f.$$

Performing the change of variables $(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},x_{4})=\sigma(y_{1},y_{2},y_{3},y_{4})$ (with unit Jacobian and the same domain of integration):

$$I=\int_{[1,2]^{4}}f(x)\,dx=\int_{[1,2]^{4}}f(\sigma(y))\,dy=\int_{[1,2]^{4}}(-f(y))\,dy=-I.$$

Hence $2I=0$, so

$$\boxed{I=0}.$$

